<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/expert/dark-matter-and-dark-energy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Everything in this atlas — every atom, star and lesson — describes <strong>about 5% of the
    universe</strong>. The other 95% is dark: <strong>dark matter</strong> (27%), whose gravity
    holds galaxies together but which no telescope has seen, and <strong>dark energy</strong>
    (68%), which accelerates the expansion and whose nature is a total mystery. This is not a
    crisis for cosmology; it is the frontier that keeps it honest.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the two darks — what we know, and the honest size of the gap" widget="equationStep" config={darkCfg} />

  <h2>How strong the evidence actually is</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dark matter is not a small correction.</strong> Rotation curves, cluster lensing, CMB acoustic peaks, structure-formation simulations — all agree matter must be ~5–6× more abundant than the visible kind, and must interact only via gravity (and maybe weak force).</li>
    <li><strong>The alternatives have been tested:</strong> modifying Newton's gravity (MOND) fits galaxies but fails clusters and the CMB. The standard picture survives; the alternative hasn't — yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Dark energy's fingerprint:</strong> the expansion's history (from supernovae + CMB + galaxy surveys) matches a constant energy density — Einstein's "cosmological constant", which he once called his biggest blunder, now the default component of the universe.</li>
    <li><strong>The detection effort:</strong> underground detectors (XENONnT, LZ) watching for rare particle collisions; the Vera Rubin Observatory surveying the sky for subtle lensing; DESI mapping 40 million galaxies. Decades of null results sharpen the question rather than closing it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Zwicky (1933); Rubin & Ford (1970); Perlmutter, Schmidt & Riess (Nobel 2011); DESI Collaboration.</p>
</TopicPage>
